# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Sizing Up the Enemy — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter Two — Sizing Up the Enemy'
HEAD = '''Five passages, and the longest chapter in the book, because the second of them disposes of six states in one breath. Marquis Wu opens with a concrete difficulty: Wei is hemmed in on every side — Qin presses from the west, Chu girdles the south, Zhao butts against the north, Qi faces the east, Yan cuts the rear, Han holds the front. “I am troubled by this. What is to be done?”

Wu Qi’s answer is not a formation. It is a table of <b>national character</b>. The men of Qi are hard, the state rich, lord and ministers arrogant and contemptuous of the common people, the government lax and its salaries unequal — so their ranks are heavy but not solid. The men of Qin are strong, their land defensible, their government severe, their rewards and punishments trustworthy, none of them willing to give way — so their ranks scatter and every man fights for himself. Chu is weak, its land wide, its government harassing and its people worn — so their ranks are orderly but do not last. Yan is honest, its people careful, fond of courage and of right and short on stratagem — so they hold their ground and will not withdraw. The Three Jin are the middle kingdoms, even-tempered and evenly governed, their people tired of war though practised in it, contemptuous of their commanders and resentful of their pay — so their ranks are well ordered and of no use. Each character is followed by the way to break it. This is the earliest enemy-character table in Chinese military literature, and it is where the <i>Wuzi</i> differs most from the <i>Sunzi</i>: Sun Wu calculates shape and momentum, Wu Qi calculates people.

The third passage changes the method. <b>Eight conditions under which you may fight without taking the auspices</b>, and <b>six under which you must withdraw without consulting them</b>. The eight are all the enemy’s exhaustion, thirst, long campaigning, spent stores, sickness, weariness, flimsiness and unformed ranks; the six are all his breadth of land, wealth of people, the affection between his high and low, his reliable rewards and clear-sighted punishments, his employment of the able and the strength of his allies. <b>Divination replaced by a checklist</b> — that is the bone of the chapter. The last two passages go finer still and reach thirteen occasions for an attack, running from an enemy newly arrived and not yet formed down to the last one, which is neither ground nor formation but fear.'''

PARA = {
 'p2-1': 'Marquis Wu said to Wu Qi: “At present Qin threatens me on the west, Chu girdles me on the south, Zhao butts against me on the north, Qi overlooks me on the east, Yan cuts off my rear and Han holds the ground in front. The troops of six states keep watch on four sides, and my position is very inconvenient. I am troubled by this. What is to be done?” (“Girdles” is literally to wrap round like a belt. Wei’s capital, first at Anyi and later at Daliang, lay in the middle of the world, with enemies on every side; this was the geography of the state and in the end the reason it declined from strength. Marquis Wu was the son of Marquis Wen. The Siku text adds a particle in “the troops of the six states.”)',
 'p2-2': '''Wu Qi replied: “The way to make a state secure is to hold forewarnedness precious. My lord is already forewarned, so calamity is far off. Let me set out for you the ways of the six states. The ranks of Qi are heavy but not solid; the ranks of Qin are scattered and fight each for itself; the ranks of Chu are orderly but do not last; the ranks of Yan hold their place and will not withdraw; the ranks of the Three Jin are well ordered and of no use. The men of Qi are hard by nature, the state is rich, lord and ministers are arrogant and extravagant and think little of the common people; the government is lax and the stipends unequal; one line of battle holds two minds, the front heavy and the rear light. Hence heavy but not solid. The way to strike them: divide your force in three, harry their right and left wings, press them so that they must follow, and their ranks can be broken. The men of Qin are strong by nature, their land is defensible, their government severe, their rewards and punishments trustworthy; none of them gives way, all have the will to fight. Hence scattered, each man fighting on his own. The way to strike them: first show them an advantage and draw them off; when the soldiers grow greedy for gain they leave their commander, and you may take them at their parting and hunt them scattered, set an ambush and seize the moment — their commander can be captured. The men of Chu are weak by nature, their land wide, their government harassing, their people worn out. Hence orderly but not lasting. The way to strike them: raid and confuse their camps, take their spirit from them first, advance lightly and fall back quickly, tire them and wear them down, and do not close with them in a pitched battle — their army can be beaten. The men of Yan are honest by nature, their people careful, fond of courage and of what is right, and poor in stratagems. Hence they hold and will not withdraw. The way to strike them: push against them and press them close, ride over them and then draw away, gallop round to their rear, so that those above grow suspicious and those below afraid; keep our chariots and horsemen carefully on the road they must shun, and their commander can be taken alive. The Three Jin are the middle kingdoms: even in temper, level in government, their people tired of war though practised in arms, contemptuous of their commanders and resentful of their small pay, with no will to die. Hence well ordered and of no use. The way to strike them: hold the difficult ground and bear down on them; when they come in force, resist; when they go, pursue; and so tire out their army. Such are the dispositions of the six. Even so, in any army there are always warriors of the tiger’s vigour — men who lift a cauldron as though it weighed nothing and outrun a war-horse, who can pull down a standard and take a commander. Men of that sort should be picked out and set apart, cherished and given honour: they are what is called the life of the army. Any of them who can handle the five weapons, whose strength and quickness are equal to it, and whose mind is set on swallowing the enemy, should be raised in rank and title, and can be used to decide a battle. Treat their parents, wives and children generously, spur them with rewards and make them fear punishment, and these will be the men who make a line of battle solid, with whom one may hold out for a long time. If you can truly reckon all this, you may attack twice your number.” Marquis Wu said: “Good.” (“Honest” renders <i>que</i>, plain and unguileful. “The life of the army” is the thread on which an army’s existence hangs. The five weapons are the dagger-axe, the staff, the halberd and the two lengths of spear; the phrase stands for weapons generally. Notice that every method of attack grows out of the character it answers: Qi is rich and proud, so divide and harry; Qin’s soldiers are greedy, so bait them; Chu’s government harasses, so wear them out; Yan will not withdraw, so go round behind; the Three Jin have no will to die, so exhaust them. Both witnesses read “pull down a standard and <i>cut down</i> a commander,” which is the standard idiom; the base text’s “take a commander” is a minority reading but construes, and is kept. Both likewise transpose two characters in “if you can truly reckon all this.” The Siku text reads “my lord is <i>first</i> forewarned” and “may <i>hold out</i> a long time” with a different particle; the Sibu congkan text miswrites “their people” as a similar-looking graph.)''',
 'p2-3': 'Wu Qi said: “In sizing up an enemy, there are eight cases in which you may engage him without taking the auspices. First: a great wind and hard cold, and they are up before dawn moving camp, breaking ice to ford water, undeterred by hardship. Second: high summer and burning heat, and they rise late and take no rest, driving on hungry and thirsty to make distance. Third: the army long in the field, its provisions gone, the common people angry and resentful, strange portents repeatedly reported and the officers unable to stop them. Fourth: their war material exhausted, firewood and fodder scarce, the weather wet day after day, nowhere left to plunder. Fifth: their numbers small, the ground and water against them, men and horses sick, no help from any neighbour. Sixth: the road long and the day late, the soldiers tired and frightened, weary and unfed, unbuckling their armour to rest. Seventh: their commanders of no weight and their officers of no seriousness, the soldiers unsteady, the army repeatedly alarmed, no other force to support it. Eighth: their ranks not yet formed and their camp not yet finished, crossing a slope or a defile with half of them hidden and half in the open. In all such cases strike, and do not hesitate. And there are six cases in which you should avoid him without consulting the auspices. First: broad lands and a numerous, wealthy population. Second: those above love those below and their kindness reaches everywhere. Third: rewards are reliable and punishments discerning, and both are dealt out at the right time. Fourth: rank follows service and offices go to the able. Fifth: many soldiers and good armour and weapons. Sixth: help from every neighbour and the backing of a great state. In any of these, if you are not their equal, avoid them and do not hesitate. This is what is meant by advancing when you see an opening and withdrawing when you know the thing is hard.” (“Up before dawn moving camp” — awake and shifting quarters. “Strange portents” are ominous freaks of nature that spread rumour through a camp. The eight are all the enemy’s present misery; the six are all the condition of his state in ordinary times. The eight are how badly he is placed today, the six are how sound he is underneath — and a sound state should not be attacked even on a bad day for it. The words “without taking the auspices” and “without consulting them” are the key to the passage: Wu Qi means to replace divination with items that can be counted. The Sibu congkan text reads “felling <i>wood</i> to ford” for “breaking ice.”)',
 'p2-4': 'Marquis Wu asked: “I wish to observe the outside of an enemy and so know his inside; to watch him advance and so know where he will stop; and so settle the question of victory. May I hear of it?” Wu Qi replied: “When the enemy comes on loosely and without forethought, his banners in confusion, his men and horses repeatedly looking back, then one of yours may strike ten of his, and you must leave him no time to act. When the feudal lords have not yet assembled, ruler and ministers are not of one mind, ditch and rampart are not yet finished, the prohibitions are not yet in force, and the whole army is in an uproar, wanting to go forward and unable, wanting to leave and not daring — then with half his numbers you may strike twice your own, and in a hundred battles you will not be in danger.” (“Repeatedly looking back” is the point of the first half: what a soldier looks back at is the road home, and which way the heads are turned tells you whether to fight. The onomatopoeia for the uproar is written with a variant graph in the Siku text. The previous passage counted the enemy’s circumstances; this one reads his appearance — and neither requires the tortoise shell.)',
 'p2-5': 'Marquis Wu asked about the occasions on which an enemy may certainly be struck. Wu Qi replied: “In using troops you must judge where the enemy is solid and where hollow, and go for the dangerous place. When the enemy has come a long way and has only just arrived and his column is not yet formed, he may be struck; when he has eaten and not yet set his guards, he may be struck; when he is on the run, he may be struck; when he is labouring, he may be struck; when he has not got the advantage of the ground, he may be struck; when he has lost his moment and his units are not following one another, he may be struck; when he has come by a long road and the rear has not rested, he may be struck; when he is half across a river, he may be struck; on a difficult road or in a narrow pass, he may be struck; when his banners move in disorder, he may be struck; when his ranks shift again and again, he may be struck; when his commander is separated from his men, he may be struck; when his heart is afraid, he may be struck. In any of these, drive a chosen edge of picked men into him, follow it with divided detachments, strike quickly and do not hesitate.” (“Lost his moment and his units not following one another” means the schedule has slipped and the formations have come apart. “Drive a chosen edge in and follow it with divided detachments” is two movements: first the breach, then the exploitation. The thirteen occasions run from the outside inward — column, food, footing, then ground, timing, river crossings — and the last of them, “when his heart is afraid,” is no longer about formations at all. It belongs with the <i>Sunzi</i>: “an army may be robbed of its spirit, a commander of his resolve.” Both witnesses insert a word — “you must <i>needs</i> judge” — which the base text lacks without loss, so the base text is kept. The Sibu congkan text puts the clause about disordered banners earlier in the list than either the base text or the Siku text does.)',
}
